    "description": "When does it make sense for a company to bring in a supply chain consultant? The warning signs, what a consultant actually delivers, and the mistake to avoid.",
    "h1": "Supply Chain Consultant: When and Why",
    "bodyHtml": "<h1>Supply Chain Consultant: When and Why</h1>\n<p>A <strong>supply chain consultant</strong> helps a company better manage the flow that runs from customer demand to delivery: forecasting, planning, inventory, procurement, and capacity. Their role isn't to replace the teams, but to give them a method and an outside perspective where habit has ended up masking the problems. The real question isn't “do we need a consultant?” but “at what point, and for what purpose?”</p>\n\n<h2>The signs that it's the right time</h2>\n<p>A few situations come up again and again.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stockouts and unsold inventory coexist: demand is not under control.</li>\n<li>Decisions are made under pressure, with no plan shared between sales and operations.</li>\n<li>The company is growing, and processes inherited from when it was smaller can no longer keep up.</li>\n<li>A major project (a new ERP, a new site, rapid growth) requires skills the team doesn't yet have in-house.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In these cases, what justifies an engagement is not the size of the company, but the gap between the complexity it has reached and the methods in place.</p>\n\n<h2>What a supply chain consultant really delivers</h2>\n<p>A good engagement leaves the company more self-sufficient than it found it. It brings a structured method, a factual diagnosis of the situation, and above all a transfer of skills: the teams must be able to run the new setup on their own once the engagement ends. It also brings a neutral perspective, free of internal habits and politics, which makes it possible to name problems the organization had come to accept as normal. A <a href=\"/en/services/supply-chain-audit\">supply chain audit</a> is often the right first step, because it establishes the facts before any larger engagement. This reflects the <strong>Two-Layer Model</strong>, which separates the decision layer from the execution layer, applied to the engagement itself: helping leadership decide better and operations execute reliably, without creating a permanent dependency on the consultant.</p>\n\n<h2>The common mistake</h2>\n<p>The costliest mistake is hiring a supply chain consultant for a deliverable (a report, a tool) instead of a capability. A thick report that ends up in a drawer changes nothing; what actually makes a difference is a team that has learned to manage differently. Before hiring, it's therefore better to define the expected outcome in terms of capability gained, not just documents produced.</p>\n\n<h2>Get a first assessment, with no commitment</h2>\n<p>Unsure whether outside help is worthwhile, or what scope it should have? Our forty-five-minute <a href=\"/diagnostic\">free diagnostic session</a> gives you an initial, factual view of your supply chain maturity, with no commitment.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What does a supply chain consultant do?</h3>\n<p>They help the company manage the flow from demand to delivery (forecasting, planning, inventory, and procurement) by bringing a method and an outside perspective.</p>\n<h3>When should a company hire a supply chain consultant?</h3>\n<p>When the company's complexity outgrows its current methods: simultaneous stockouts and unsold inventory, decisions made under pressure, growth, or a major project.</p>\n<h3>Does a supply chain consultant create dependency?</h3>\n<p>A good engagement does the opposite: it transfers the skills so the teams can run the setup on their own after the engagement ends.</p>",
