    "description": "In-house supply chain training builds your teams' skills on your own processes. Who it's for and how it works.",
    "h1": "In-House Supply Chain Training",
    "bodyHtml": "<h1>In-House Supply Chain Training</h1>\n<p><strong>In-house supply chain training</strong> means training a team directly on the methods and processes of its own business, rather than in a classroom with generic case studies. The difference is decisive: instead of learning concepts in the abstract, participants work on their real flows, their real constraints, and their real numbers. The skills acquired stay within the company and can be applied the very next day.</p>\n\n<h2>Who supply chain training is for</h2>\n<p>This format is aimed primarily at teams who run operations without having had structured training: sales, purchasing, production, or logistics managers, as well as executives who want to speak the same language as their teams. The goal isn't to train specialists, but to give generalists the reference points that make their decisions sounder.</p>\n\n<h2>How supply chain training works</h2>\n<h3>1. Scoping</h3>\n<p>The skills that are actually useful in your context are identified: forecasting, inventory management, planning, and process improvement. Whatever doesn't serve the company today is set aside.</p>\n<h3>2. Workshops on your own cases</h3>\n<p>Skills are built through workshops applied to your own processes, alternating between method and immediate hands-on practice. Participants learn by doing, using the company's own material.</p>\n<h3>3. Embedding</h3>\n<p>Lasting reference points are left behind (materials, procedures, and metrics) so that the skills outlast the training and don't depend on participants' memory.</p>\n\n<h2>When should a company choose in-house training?</h2>\n<p>In-house training makes sense as soon as several people need to build the same skills, or when operational knowledge rests with a handful of individuals. Training the team turns individual knowledge into collective capability: the “execution” layer of the <strong>Two-Layer Model</strong>, made solid and shared. A sound decision at the top is only as good as the team's mastery of the methods needed to carry it out.</p>\n\n<h2>The common mistake</h2>\n<p>The common mistake is choosing generic training, disconnected from the company's context: participants come away with concepts they don't know how to apply to their daily work. The other pitfall is training without embedding anything (no materials, no procedures), so the skills fade within a few weeks. Useful training is measured by what it leaves behind in the company, not by the number of hours delivered.</p>\n\n<h2>Build a tailored skills plan</h2>\n<p>Want to identify which skills your team should strengthen first? Our forty-five-minute <a href=\"/diagnostic\">free diagnostic session</a> helps frame a skills development plan tailored to your context.</p>\n\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What is in-house supply chain training?</h3>\n<p>It's training delivered within the company that builds a team's skills directly on the methods and processes of its own business, rather than on generic case studies.</p>\n<h3>Who is in-house supply chain training for?</h3>\n<p>Teams running operations (sales, purchasing, production, and logistics) and executives who want to share the same methodological language.</p>\n<h3>How does it differ from a traditional classroom course?</h3>\n<p>In-house training relies on your own flows, constraints, and numbers; the skills acquired stay within the company and apply immediately.</p>",
